Recall that we let $\mathfrak{G}$ denote the grid. Let $S\not=\emptyset$

be a finite set of tile symbols. We let $\mathfrak{G}_S$ denote the class of structures $\mathfrak{A}$ that satisfy the following conditions.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The structure $\mathfrak{A}$ is an expansion of the grid $\mathfrak{G}$ to the vocabulary $\{V,H\}\cup S$.
\item Each point in the domain of $\mathfrak{A}$ is in the extension of exactly one predicate symbol $P_t\in S$.
\end{enumerate} 
Structures in the class $\mathfrak{G}_S$ are called \emph{$S$-labelled grids}.


Similarly, we let $\mathcal{G}_S$ denote the class of structures $\mathfrak{A}$ that satisfy the following conditions.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The structure $\mathfrak{A}$ is an expansion of some supergrid to the vocabulary $\{V,H\}\cup S$.
\item Each point in the domain of $\mathfrak{A}$ is in the extension of exactly one predicate symbol $P_t\in S$.
\end{enumerate} 
Structures in the class $\mathcal{G}_S$ are called
\emph{$S$-labelled supergrids}.






The class of \emph{$S$-labelled recurrence grids} is defined in the obvious way.

